---
import { Code } from 'astro:components'
import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.astro'

const modeExample = `import { ActivationGuardProvider } from '@sparsh/react'

<ActivationGuardProvider
  mode={{ age: 'report' }}
  policies={['age', 'continuity', 'semantics', 'doubleFire']}
>
  <Application />
</ActivationGuardProvider>`

const stableIdentityExample = `<ul>
  {invoices.map((invoice) => (
    <li key={invoice.id} data-guard-key={invoice.id}>
      <button onClick={() => openInvoice(invoice.id)}>
        Open {invoice.name}
      </button>
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>`
---

<DocsShell
  title="Core concepts"
  description="Learn what sparsh observes, how the policies decide, and how to tune the guard without breaking expected input."
>
  <section class="docs-section" id="interaction-model">
    <p class="section-kicker">INTERACTION MODEL</p>
    <h2>Protect the intent behind an activation</h2>
    <p>
      A user begins an interaction against the screen they can perceive. Before the browser commits
      the click or key activation, an interface update may move the target, replace its identity, or
      change what it means. sparsh records intent and a target snapshot, then compares the snapshot
      at activation time. A suspicious activation can be reported or stopped according to mode.
    </p>
    <ol class="docs-list">
      <li><strong>Resolve:</strong> map the raw event target to the nearest interactive element.</li>
      <li><strong>Snapshot:</strong> record its perceivable age, position, identity, and semantic fingerprint.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluate:</strong> run applicable policies and produce one decision.</li>
      <li><strong>Respond:</strong> allow the activation or cancel it, then emit callbacks for application feedback.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="modes">
    <p class="section-kicker">MODES</p>
    <h2>Enforce by default; use report to tune</h2>
    <p>
      The default mode is <code>enforce</code>. In <code>report</code> mode, policies still evaluate
      and callbacks still fire, but the activation is allowed through. You can set one global mode
      or override modes by policy.
    </p>
    <Code code={modeExample} lang="tsx" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
      When a mode map is supplied, any omitted policy remains <code>enforce</code>. The first
      applicable policy that returns a suspicious verdict determines the decision; a report-mode
      verdict lets that activation through and ends evaluation for it. Consider policy order and
      overlap when mixing modes.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Decision field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>allowed</code></td><td>The first suspicious policy verdict; false means a policy flagged the activation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>enforced</code></td><td>True only when that verdict was in enforce mode and sparsh stopped the activation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>policy</code></td><td>The policy that produced the verdict; absent when all applicable policies allow.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>reason</code></td><td>Human-readable explanation for a suspicious verdict, when provided.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>event</code></td><td>Input kind and activation phase.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>target</code></td><td>Identity summary, perceivable age, and optional stable guard key.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="policies">
    <p class="section-kicker">POLICY REFERENCE</p>
    <h2>Four independent signals</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it checks</th><th>Inputs</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>age</code></td><td>Target has been perceivable for at least <code>cooldownMs</code>.</td><td>Pointer, keyboard, virtual</td><td>Enforce; threshold 500 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>continuity</code></td><td>Target identity and rectangle stayed stable from pointer down to pointer up.</td><td>Pointer</td><td>Enforce; tolerance 4 px</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>semantics</code></td><td>Meaningful target properties did not change during the interaction.</td><td>All kinds; needs a stored intent snapshot</td><td>Enforce; text comparison off</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>doubleFire</code></td><td>A rapid residual press did not land on a new control at overlapping coordinates.</td><td>Pointer</td><td>Enforce; 300 ms window</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3>Age</h3>
    <p>
      Age measures time since an element became perceivable, not simply when its DOM node was
      created. A target younger than <code>cooldownMs</code> is flagged. Unknown or pre-existing
      targets are treated as old (infinite age), which fails open. Age also applies to keyboard and
      virtual activations; if the threshold catches legitimate fast keyboard use, configure Age in
      report mode or tune the cooldown.
    </p>
    <h3>Continuity</h3>
    <p>
      Continuity compares identity and position across the pointer press. It does not run for
      keyboard or virtual activations because they have no pointer-down snapshot.
    </p>
    <h3>Semantics</h3>
    <p>
      The default fingerprint checks disabled state, accessible label, role, link target or control
      value, and <code>data-guard-key</code>. Text comparison is opt-in because counters, timestamps,
      and live labels can change during an otherwise valid interaction. The current DOM host records
      semantic intent on pointer down; without a stored intent snapshot, Semantics allows the
      activation. Age still evaluates the target’s current age for keyboard and virtual input.
    </p>
    <h3>DoubleFire</h3>
    <p>
      This pointer-only policy looks for a different, newly visible target at overlapping
      coordinates shortly after an allowed pointer activation. A quick repeat on the same stable
      control is allowed; this policy is not a general double-click debounce.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="stable-identity">
    <p class="section-kicker">DYNAMIC LISTS</p>
    <h2>Give reused rows a stable identity</h2>
    <p>
      A framework key helps the renderer reconcile nodes, but it is not automatically available to
      the DOM host. Add <code>data-guard-key</code> when one physical row can represent different
      entities after a reorder or update.
    </p>
    <Code code={stableIdentityExample} lang="tsx" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
      sparsh reads the nearest ancestor’s key. Without it, a recycled DOM element whose content
      changes in place may look like the same target, especially while text comparison is disabled.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="accessibility">
    <p class="section-kicker">INPUT AND ACCESSIBILITY</p>
    <h2>Keyboard and assistive technology remain part of the model</h2>
    <p>
      The DOM host observes Pointer Events and activation keys Enter and Space. It does not intercept
      Escape, Tab, focus changes, scrolling, or unrelated keys. Continuity and DoubleFire are
      pointer-only. Age evaluates keyboard and virtual activations; Semantics only compares meaning
      when it has a stored intent snapshot. Report mode is available if Age needs tuning for fast
      keyboard users.
    </p>
    <p>
      A block should be explained. Use <code>onDecision</code> or a per-element hook to show a
      status message, preserve focus, and invite the user to try again. <code>onBlocked</code> is
      optional visual feedback, not a replacement for an accessible explanation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" id="limits">
    <p class="section-kicker">KNOWN LIMITS</p>
    <h2>Signals are intentionally conservative</h2>
    <ul class="docs-list">
      <li>Opacity-only reveals on an element that already exists are not detected by the current age tracking.</li>
      <li>An overlay can cover an unmoved target without changing its resolved identity; occlusion is not generally detected.</li>
      <li>Age treats unknown/pre-existing targets as old and allows them, favoring fewer false positives.</li>
      <li>In-place entity changes need <code>data-guard-key</code> for reliable identity comparison.</li>
      <li>Exceptions in policy evaluation fail open. Visual callback exceptions are ignored and do not affect cancellation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For binding-specific APIs, see the <a href="/docs/react/">React</a>, <a href="/docs/vue/">Vue</a>, and <a href="/docs/dom/">DOM</a> references.</p>
  </section>
</DocsShell>
